STORY DNA — 12 CRIME NON-NEGOTIABLES
================================================================================

 1. VICTIM-FIRST STRUCTURE — The victim must be humanized within the first
    90 seconds. No crime detail before emotional investment in the victim.

 2. DECEPTION ARCHITECTURE — Every crime story must have at least one false
    cover story that the viewer briefly believes before it's dismantled.
    The cover story's collapse is the structural climax.

 3. OBJECT HOOK — One everyday physical object that becomes the pivot point
    of the story. Introduce it early, pay it off in the investigation.
    (Mutton soup / blue drum / watermelon / hidden jewelry / mangalsutra in room)

 4. DIGITAL TRAIL CINEMATICS — Social media / WhatsApp / CDR is not a data
    dump. Each digital clue is a dramatic scene with dialogue and reaction.

 5. FORENSIC SPECIFICITY — Name the exact test, the exact lab, the exact
    finding, the exact number of rounds (54 बार FSL टेस्ट). Science creates trust.

 6. INSTITUTIONAL ANGLE — Every strong crime story has a system that failed:
    corrupt police / POSH committee suppressed complaints / evidence destroyed /
    VIP identity protected. Name the failure specifically.

 7. PERPETRATOR'S BEST MOMENT — The killer must appear to be winning at least
    once in the second half. Media believing them / police misled / public
    sympathizing. Victory without this moment feels cheap.

 8. VICTIM REFRAIN — Return to the victim every 3–4 paragraphs via an object,
    a habit, a dream they had. The viewer must grieve, not just be shocked.

 9. SOCIAL COMMENT — End with one social truth the case reveals:
    (बाल विवाह / digital deception / VIP immunity / workplace predation /
     women's safety at resorts / family honour over child's life)

10. DIALOGUE MINIMUM — 1 line of dialogue per 1,000 characters. Every
    dialogue line must do one of: reveal character under pressure /
    deliver ultimatum / show dramatic irony (viewer knows more than speaker).

11. INDIA FRAMING — Victim dignity is the frame. Not sensationalism.
    Name the crime, not the community. State facts without generalization.
    Where institutional failure exists, name the institution.

12. END CTA — Like + subscribe + weekly upload mention. ONLY at very end.
    ❌ No CTA interruption in the middle of the script (unlike reference
       transcripts which break for course ads — do NOT replicate this).

================================================================================
THE SENTENCE RHYTHM SYSTEM
================================================================================

Sentence length controls viewer emotional state.

SHORT sentences (4–8 words) = shock, discovery, arrest, revelation
MEDIUM sentences (9–18 words) = building tension, investigation, context
LONG sentences (19–30 words) = atmosphere, backstory, emotional weight

RULES:
  • Crime revelation moments: SHORT sentences only.
    Wrong: "जब पुलिस ने उस ड्रम को पोस्टमार्टम हाउस भेजा और उसे काटा..."
    Right: "ड्रम काटा। सीमेंट हटाई। और फिर पुलिस वालों ने जो देखा —
            वह उन्होंने पहले कभी नहीं देखा था।"

  • Investigation scenes: mix medium and short — never 4 long in a row.
  • Victim humanization: LONG sentences — let grief breathe.
  • Climax sequence: SHORT → MEDIUM → SHORT → SHORT → LONG (payoff exhale)
  • Paragraph length: 3–6 sentences. Never more than 7.
    If a paragraph exceeds 7 sentences, split it — new visual scene.

================================================================================
THE DIALOGUE SYSTEM
================================================================================

RULES:
  • Minimum 1 line of dialogue per 1,000 characters.
  • Dialogue must ALWAYS do one of three things:
      (a) Reveal character under pressure (killer's cold response / victim's fear)
      (b) Deliver ultimatum or decision (confession / threat / last request)
      (c) Create dramatic irony (viewer knows perpetrator is lying)

  • NEVER use dialogue for information delivery alone.
    Wrong: "उसने पुलिस को बताया कि वह 26 नवंबर को 5 बजे घर से निकली थी।"
    Right: "उसने एक पल भी नहीं रुककर जवाब दिया — '26 नवंबर? हां। हम साथ
            ही बाहर गए थे। रास्ते में हमला हुआ।' पुलिस ने नोट किया।"

  • CRIME-SPECIFIC DIALOGUE TYPES:
    Killer playing victim: ठंडा, तैयार, परफेक्ट स्क्रिप्ट
    Real victim's last words / chats: innocent, scared, unaware
    Officer's realization moment: one quiet line that changes everything
    Family's grief: raw, incomplete sentences, disbelief

  • Reconstructed dialogue is allowed. Do not add disclaimers.
  • Never write more than 4 lines of continuous dialogue without
    a beat of narration in between.

================================================================================
THE SPECIFICITY RULE — NUMBERS CREATE TRUST
================================================================================

MANDATORY specifics in crime stories:
  • Exact dates: "5 जनवरी 2021 की उस सुबह"
  • Exact times: "रात के 2:00 बज रहे थे"
  • Exact amounts: "₹1 लाख की सुपारी / ₹3 लाख के गहने / ₹44,000 की कैब"
  • Exact forensic: "54 बार FSL टेस्ट / 12 इंच गहरा घाव / एक सटीक वार"
  • Exact counts: "9 FIR / 147 employees / 40 दिन का ऑपरेशन / 100 गवाह"
  • Exact technology: "जिंक फास्फाइड / फास्फीन गैस / CDR / DNA / CCTV"
  • IPC/BNS sections: "BNS धारा 103 / धारा 238 / POSH Act"
  • Distances / geography: "घर से 15-20 किमी दूर वरकला / 50 किमी दूर सोहरा"

BAD:  "पुलिस ने जांच की और सच पता चला।"
GOOD: "साइबर सेल ने प्रियंका का डिलीट किया हुआ CDR रिकवर किया।
       उसमें एक नंबर था जिस पर हत्या की रात और उससे पहले
       चार दिनों तक रात-रात भर बातें हुई थी।"

================================================================================
THE KILLER / PERPETRATOR RULE
================================================================================

The strength of the story's payoff = the strength of the deception.

REQUIREMENTS:
  • Killer must have a PEAK DECEPTION MOMENT in the second half:
    a point where their cover story is working, they appear to be winning.
  • Killer must be shown as SMART at least twice — not just cruel.
  • Killer's downfall must come from a specific tactical error or a small
    detail they overlooked — not just "police was better."
  • Killer must have one humanizing detail that makes the betrayal feel real
    (a mother who loved her, a husband who trusted her, a child left behind).

KILLER ARC FOR CRIME STORIES:
  Normal life / relationship → hidden motive develops → planning phase →
  crime execution → cover story deployed → appears to succeed →
  ONE overlooked detail → investigation pivot → cover story collapses →
  arrest → systemic meaning

KILLER DECEPTION TYPES (from reference stories):
  Type 1: Crying victim act (Priyanka — roped herself, cried on camera)
  Type 2: Cooperative informant (Pulkit — filed missing report himself)
  Type 3: Digital ghost (Muskan — sent fake messages from husband's phone)
  Type 4: Substitute body (Swati — brought another man as "husband")
  Type 5: False backstory victim (Sonam — played the trapped daughter)

================================================================================
THE EMOTIONAL ARCHITECTURE — 8 BEATS IN ORDER
================================================================================

Every crime script must deliver these 8 emotions, in this sequence:

  1. SHOCK          — Cold open. Something ordinary revealed as sinister.
  2. CURIOSITY      — Who is this person? What led here? How was this hidden?
  3. EMPATHY        — Care about the victim. Their life. Their dream. Their family.
  4. DREAD          — The plan might not be discovered. The killer is too smart.
  5. TENSION        — Investigation is happening. One wrong piece and it fails.
  6. CATHARSIS      — The cover story breaks. The arrest. The confession.
  7. JUSTICE        — Named, convicted, judged. Victim acknowledged.
  8. REFLECTION     — The systemic question that stays with the viewer.

RULES:
  • DREAD must come BEFORE TENSION. Dread = "they might get away with it."
    Tension = "the police are closing in right now."
  • EMPATHY (beat 3) must be established before DREAD (beat 4).
    If viewer doesn't care about the victim, the killer escaping feels abstract.
  • REFLECTION (beat 8) is NOT the CTA. It comes before the CTA.
    It is the social / moral question the case leaves behind.

================================================================================
THE LOOP-BACK SYSTEM — CRIME VERSION
================================================================================

Write the cold open AND the ending together. They must be designed as a pair.

Cold open → creates unanswered question.
Ending → answers it completely AND names the human cost.

CRIME LOOP-BACK EXAMPLES from reference stories:

  COLD OPEN: "वो ड्रम उठ ही नहीं रहा था। मजदूरों ने ढक्कन खोला।
              और जो बदबू आई — वह इंसान की थी।"
  ENDING:    "सौरभ राजपूत। 32 साल। लंदन की बेकरी में पसीना बहाकर
              अपनी बच्ची के लिए पैसे भेजता था।
              उसी घर में। उसी बेड के नीचे। 15 टुकड़ों में।
              और उस रात उसी बेड पर मुस्कान चैन की नींद सोई।"

  COLD OPEN: "प्रियंका रो रही थी। मीडिया के कैमरे थे।
              पूरा देश उसे बेचारी समझ रहा था।"
  ENDING:    "36 घंटे। धार पुलिस को बस इतना वक्त लगा।
              उस घर में जो छुपाया था — वो गहने — वो सोने के कंगन —
              वो उसी अलमारी में थे। बाहर कोई चोर नहीं था।
              दुश्मन उन्हीं चार दीवारों के अंदर था।"

