================================================================================
SCENE WRITING — 2D ANIMATION READY
================================================================================

Each paragraph = one drawable scene.

CRIME-SPECIFIC VISUAL SCENES:
  Investigation:
    — Police officer at table with CDR printout, fingers pointing to a name
    — FSL lab: white-coated scientist, test tubes, green/black internal organs
    — Interrogation room: single light, two chairs, suspect's hands shaking
    — CCTV still on laptop screen, cursor circling a face
    — Postmortem table: covered body, doctor noting one precise wound

  Crime scene:
    — Blue drum in corner of dark room, crack of light under door
    — Watermelon cut open on plate, red flesh, powder residue barely visible
    — Syringe on bathroom shelf, sleeping pills in powder form in food
    — Locked almirah with jewelry inside — found, not stolen
    — Mangalsutra on nightstand — left behind deliberately

  Digital scenes:
    — Phone screen with fake Facebook profile (blurred face, generic name)
    — WhatsApp conversation — bubble by bubble appearing (not a screenshot)
    — Deleted chat — gray interface, recovery software showing fragments
    — CDR log — two columns: TIME | NUMBER. One number circled red.

  Family grief:
    — Old photograph on a shelf
    — Empty chair at dining table
    — School bag hanging on hook, owner never coming back

VISUAL ANCHOR RULE:
Every investigation or evidence scene must contain a physical visual anchor.
No abstract description for more than 3 sentences without:
  CDR / DNA report / CCTV still / FSL table / FIR form / syringe /
  fingerprint card / blood sample / postmortem room / digital chat screen.

================================================================================
GORE & SENSITIVITY RULES — CRIME EDITION
================================================================================

  - Violence details: state the fact, not the sensation.
    Wrong: "उसके सिर से खून की धारा बह रही थी..."
    Right: "देवृष्ण के सिर पर एक सटीक, गहरा वार था। एक ही बार।
            काम तमाम।"

  - Body discovery: describe through the reaction of the discoverer,
    not through graphic description of the body itself.
    "मजदूरों ने ढक्कन खोला। एक पल रुके। फिर वहां से भागे।"
    Not: "कटा हुआ सर... टुकड़े..."

  - Child victims: never graphic. State loss through the living relatives.
    "ज़ैनब 12 साल की थी। घर की सबसे चुलबुली बच्ची।
     अगली सुबह वो नहीं उठी।"

  - Sexual violence reference: acknowledge the crime, do not dramatize.
    State FIR sections, support agencies, impact on victim — not act details.

  - Communal / religious angle: if the case has a religious motive (TCS case),
    state the facts as reported in FIR. Do not extrapolate to communities.
    "आरोपियों ने धर्म परिवर्तन का दबाव डाला — यह FIR में दर्ज है।"
    Never: "[community] के लोग ऐसा करते हैं।"

================================================================================
VOICE RULES — CRIME EDITION
================================================================================

DEFAULT:
Strong declarative — narrator was in every interrogation room, every lab,
every WhatsApp conversation.

✅ "पुलिस ने 40 दिन उस ऑफिस के अंदर रहकर सब देखा।"
✅ "CDR में एक नंबर था। उसी नंबर ने सब खोल दिया।"
❌ "पुलिस के मुताबिक..." on every single line.

Conversational hooks, sparingly:
  • "जरा सोचिए..."
  • "ध्यान दीजिए इस शब्द पर..."
  • "यह बात याद रखिए — यही सब कुछ बदल देगी।"
  • "कल्पना कीजिए उस परिवार की..."

Hindi: accessible, not Sanskritised.
English terms allowed where natural:
  CDR / DNA / CCTV / FIR / POSH / FSL / SIT / NIA / ATS /
  confession / interrogation / forensic / digital trail / cyber cell

Not a news bulletin. Not a lecture on society.
Cinematic, urgent, visually alive, victim-centered.

================================================================================
CRIME SUB-GENRE NOTES
================================================================================

DOMESTIC MURDER (Priyanka, Mutton Soup, Blue Drum)
  → Trust inside home, familiar routine used as weapon, household objects
    as murder tools, cover story targeting family sympathy, CDR/DNA reveal.

DIGITAL DECEPTION (Facebook Murder, TCS)
  → Fake identity, chat screenshots, platform mechanics, digital recovery,
    psychological manipulation over time, victim's growing isolation.

LOVE TRIANGLE / HONEYMOON MURDER (Meghalaya, Blue Drum)
  → Prior relationship hidden, new partner as tool/weapon, travel as
    alibi cover, phone location data, cooperative killer playing victim.

CONTRACT KILL (Priyanka Purohit)
  → Advance payment, supari killer, planning phase with WhatsApp,
    perfect crime rehearsal, forensic gaps in staged robbery.

CORPORATE / INSTITUTIONAL CRIME (TCS Nashik, Ankita Bhandari)
  → Power hierarchy exploited, HR system corrupted, complaint suppression,
    VIP angle, undercover police operation, national security angle.

POISON / UNSOLVED (Mumbai Watermelon)
  → Forensic science as hero, 54-round testing, organ evidence, motive
    theory (witness silencing), unsolved = leave viewer with open question.

